<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Birendra of Nepal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a>–<a href="/years/2001">2001</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as King Birendra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Birendra (1945–2001) was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> from 1972 to 2001. He ruled at first through the partyless panchayat system, but the People's Movement of 1990 forced him to legalise political parties and accept a constitution making him a constitutional monarch. He was killed with his queen, Aishwarya, and other members of the royal family in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narayanhiti-palace">Narayanhiti Palace</a> massacre of 1 June 2001.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/birendra-of-nepal.jpg" alt="Birendra of Nepal" width="320" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CAPTAIN MEDUSA · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Birendra_Bir_Bikram_Shah_c._1967_(restoration).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Birendra was born on 29 December 1945 at the Narayanhiti Royal Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>, the son of Crown Prince Mahendra and Crown Princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a>. An earlier son of Mahendra, Rabindra Shah, was born to an unmarried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurung">Gurung</a> woman and counted as illegitimate, so Birendra became heir apparent. He studied at St Joseph's School, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> school in Darjeeling, then at Eton College from 1959 to 1964, and later at the University of Tokyo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard University</a>. On 27 February 1970 he married Aishwarya Rajya Lakshmi Devi, his second cousin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Birendra came to the throne on 31 January 1972 after Mahendra's death, and was crowned on 24 February 1975 after a mourning year and a year astrologers judged inauspicious. He inherited a country where political parties were banned and rule ran through panchayat councils, so that he was effectively an absolute monarch. Growing pro-democracy demands led him to call a referendum between a party-less and a multi-party system in May 1980. The party-less system won by 55 per cent to 45, a result that democracy campaigners later claimed was rigged.</p><p>In 1990 strikes and pro-democracy riots led him to lift the ban on parties and agree to become a constitutional monarch. He promulgated a new constitution on 9 November 1990, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> with multiparty democracy. A civil war between Maoist rebels and government forces began in 1996 and lasted until 2006. His government set up national parks, among them Chitwan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-everest">Sagarmatha</a>, and he promoted Nepal as a zone of peace at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, a proposal 116 countries supported.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 1 June 2001 Birendra and his immediate family were shot dead at dinner at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narayanhiti-palace">Narayanhiti Palace</a> by his son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dipendra-of-nepal">Crown Prince Dipendra</a>. Dipendra shot himself and, in a coma, was proclaimed king, but died a few days later. Birendra's younger brother Gyanendra then became king. Monuments named for Birendra were renamed after the political changes of 2006 and the end of the monarchy in 2008.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Birendra of Nepal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The first Jana Andolan (People’s Movement) of 1990 forced <b>King Birendra</b> to lift the ban on political parties and accept a constitution making him a constitutional monarch.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=27.71,85.32&amp;year=1981" aria-label="Kathmandu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Birendra of Nepal"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4646 5189h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4646 5189h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=27.71,85.32&amp;year=1981">What was here in 1981? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
